CHECKS AND THRESHOLDS
  C  contrast      4.5:1 for normal text, 3.0:1 for large text (>=28 px, or >=24 px bold).
                   Measured against the nearest ancestor Image with alpha >= 0.50, composited over black,
                   which is the darkest plausible backdrop. For light text that is the most favourable case.
  L  label fit     rect.height >= fontSize x 1.12. Split into 'clipped' (overflow truncates: the label can vanish)
                   and 'spills' (overflow allowed: it draws outside its box). Clipped fails, spills warns.
  T  touch target  96 x 96 canvas px, for every Button and InputField.
     Derivation, stated rather than assumed: on a 1080p landscape phone (1920x1080) the scaler above gives a uniform
     scale of 1.50 (1920/1280 = 1080/720 = 1.5). Android's 48 dp minimum touch target at xxhdpi (3.0 density) is
     144 physical px, and 144 / 1.5 = 96 canvas px. This is a canvas-px measurement against a reference resolution on
     an assumed phone class - NOT a measurement taken with a ruler on a handset.
  S  type size     a caption on a Button >= 20 px; any other label >= 18 px.   [Week 15]
     These two numbers are a PROJECT DECISION, not a WCAG requirement: the standard asks for 200% resize (SC 1.4.4),
     which a fixed-size uGUI canvas cannot do at all. 20 px at the 1.5 scale above is ~13.3 dp of text, and 18 px is
     ~12 dp; both are floors chosen so no string on screen is smaller than the caption of the control it sits on.
  N  non-text      a control's own fill >= 3.0:1 against the panel behind it. WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.11.   [Week 15]
     Measured as the button's Image colour composited over the nearest opaque ancestor panel, against that same panel
     composited over black. A control whose fill cannot be told from its panel has no visible edge, whatever its label
     contrast. Inputs whose own Image is translucent or absent are reported as not measurable, not as passes.
  Not measurable   a label with no opaque backdrop (drawn over the 3D board) has no contrast ratio. It is reported as
                   not measurable rather than given an invented number.
